module sysl.crypto
@no_alloc

// Everything about SHA-2 that depends on how wide a word is, and nothing else. The four digests
// are two algorithms: SHA-224 and SHA-256 are the same 32-bit compression started from different
// initial values and truncated differently, and SHA-384 and SHA-512 are the same 64-bit one. So
// the width is what a type parameter carries, and the rest — which initial values, how many bytes
// to publish — travels as an argument.
//
// The round constants are the first 32 or 64 bits of the fractional parts of the cube roots of the
// first 64 (or 80) primes; the SHA-256 and SHA-512 initial values are the square roots of the
// first eight. SHA-224's and SHA-384's initial values are not derived that way — they are the
// published tables, chosen so that a truncated digest cannot be mistaken for a prefix of the
// untruncated one.

// The rotation the mixing functions are built out of. This used to be a generic `rotr` written here
// — `(x >> n) | (x << (T.width() - n))`, which is the expression everybody writes and which is
// undefined at `n = 0`, since shifting by the whole width is. SHA-2 never rotates by zero, so the
// version that was here worked; a reader copying it into something that does would have found out
// the hard way. `rotate_right` is the machine's own instruction and is defined at every amount.
import sysl.math.Bits

private[crypto] val k256: [64]u32 = [
        0x428a2f98, 0x71374491, 0xb5c0fbcf, 0xe9b5dba5,
        0x3956c25b, 0x59f111f1, 0x923f82a4, 0xab1c5ed5,
        0xd807aa98, 0x12835b01, 0x243185be, 0x550c7dc3,
        0x72be5d74, 0x80deb1fe, 0x9bdc06a7, 0xc19bf174,
        0xe49b69c1, 0xefbe4786, 0x0fc19dc6, 0x240ca1cc,
