// What the compression needs to know about its word type. The first two are facts about `u32` and
// `u64` rather than about any particular word — the round count and the two constant tables — so
// they are declared with **no receiver** and reached through the type: `T.word_rounds()` inside
// anything bounded by `Word`, and `u32.word_rounds()` from outside. The rest act on a word and take
// one.
//
// The arithmetic is **required** rather than repeated. `Word` is a promise the trait itself makes,
// so `[T: Word]` licenses every operator below without naming one of them, and `impl Word for u32`
// is held to arithmetic `u32` has always had.
//
// **`Zero` is required for the same reason and used to be written out here.** The schedule needs a
// word to start from and there was no way to ask an integer type for one, so this trait declared its
// own `zero()` and both blocks below answered it with a literal. The core trait covers every integer
// width now, so requiring it says the same thing and the two blocks say nothing at all — `T.zero()`
// in the compression is the standard module's member, reached through the bound like every operator
// beside it.
//
// **`Bits` is the same story told twice, and the width was the second telling.** This trait declared
// a `word_bits()` of its own for exactly the reason it once declared a `zero()`: there was no way to
// ask an integer type how wide it is. `Bits.width()` is that way, so requiring `Bits` says what
// `word_bits()` said and the two blocks below say nothing at all — `T.width()` in the compression is
// `sysl.math`'s member, reached through the bound like `T.zero()` beside it.
private[crypto] trait Word: Add + BitAnd + BitOr + BitXor + Bits + Not + Shl + Shr + Sub + Zero
    word_rounds() -> usize

    round_key(i: usize) -> Self

    // The four mixing functions. They differ between the widths only in how far they rotate, which
    // is why they sit here rather than in the compression: the rotate amounts are as much a fact
    // about the width as the round count is.
    bsig0(self) -> Self
    bsig1(self) -> Self
    ssig0(self) -> Self
    ssig1(self) -> Self

impl Word for u32
    word_rounds() -> usize = 64

    round_key(i: usize) -> u32 = k256[i]

    bsig0(self) -> u32 = self.rotate_right(2) ^ self.rotate_right(13) ^ self.rotate_right(22)
    bsig1(self) -> u32 = self.rotate_right(6) ^ self.rotate_right(11) ^ self.rotate_right(25)
    ssig0(self) -> u32 = self.rotate_right(7) ^ self.rotate_right(18) ^ (self >> 3)
    ssig1(self) -> u32 = self.rotate_right(17) ^ self.rotate_right(19) ^ (self >> 10)

impl Word for u64
    word_rounds() -> usize = 80

    round_key(i: usize) -> u64 = k512[i]

    bsig0(self) -> u64 = self.rotate_right(28) ^ self.rotate_right(34) ^ self.rotate_right(39)
    bsig1(self) -> u64 = self.rotate_right(14) ^ self.rotate_right(18) ^ self.rotate_right(41)
    ssig0(self) -> u64 = self.rotate_right(1) ^ self.rotate_right(8) ^ (self >> 7)
    ssig1(self) -> u64 = self.rotate_right(19) ^ self.rotate_right(61) ^ (self >> 6)
